\section{Sources}{

\code{con} must be a \link[DBI:dbConnect]{DBI} connection to DuckDB (packages
DBI and duckdb). Give either \code{table}, the name of a table or view (a
character string or a \code{\link[DBI:Id]{DBI::Id()}}), or \code{sql}, a \code{SELECT} query. A
character vector of Parquet paths (globs allowed) opens a private
in-memory DuckDB connection that reads them with \code{read_parquet()}; it is
closed when the design is garbage collected.
}

\section{Training and new data}{

With a formula, the design is learned from the remote data, as
\code{design_matrix(formula, data)} learns it from a data frame:
\itemize{
\item Levels: those of an \code{ENUM} column (a factor in R) are its declared
levels; those of a character column are its distinct values in the rows
used, sorted as \code{\link[=factor]{factor()}} sorts them in R (not by SQL collation).
Logical columns are factors with levels \code{FALSE} and \code{TRUE}.
\item Rows: rows with a missing value (SQL \code{NULL}, or \code{NaN}) in any model
variable, response included, are dropped, as \code{\link[stats:na.omit]{stats::na.omit()}} drops
them.
\item Transformations whose parameters depend on the data, such as
\code{\link[stats:poly]{stats::poly()}}, \code{\link[splines:ns]{splines::ns()}} or \code{\link[=scale]{scale()}}, are fitted as
\code{\link[stats:model.frame]{stats::model.frame()}} fits them, on the whole of their input columns:
those columns (only those) are read into R once. To avoid this, fit
the specification in R, on a sample or on other data, and pass it
instead of the formula.
\item Variables computed by an R expression that yields a factor, such as
\code{factor(cyl)} or \code{interaction(a, b)}, take the levels that the
expression gives on the distinct values of its input columns.
}

With a specification (a design, a plan, a fitted model or another remote
design), the levels, contrasts and transformation parameters come from
it, and the remote data is only scored. Levels absent from the
specification raise the error that \code{\link[stats:model.frame]{stats::model.frame()}} raises, and
columns of another type than in training (a numeric variable stored as
text, say) the error of \code{\link[stats:predict.lm]{stats::predict.lm()}}. As in memory, the rows
with missing values are kept by default (\code{na.action = na.pass}): their
predictions are \code{NULL}, and products involving their columns are \code{NA}.
With \code{na.action = na.omit}, they are dropped.
}

\section{Engine}{

Products with a remote design are computed in DuckDB. SQL computes
factors, character and logical columns, numeric columns, interactions,
and numeric expressions built from \code{+}, \code{-}, \code{*}, \code{/}, \code{^}, \code{\link[=I]{I()}}, \code{\link[=log]{log()}}
(with base 2, 10 or a number), \code{\link[=log2]{log2()}}, \code{\link[=log10]{log10()}}, \code{\link[=exp]{exp()}}, \code{\link[=sqrt]{sqrt()}} and
\code{\link[=abs]{abs()}}. Any other variable, such as \code{\link[stats:poly]{stats::poly()}}, \code{\link[splines:ns]{splines::ns()}},
\code{factor(cyl)} or a function of yours, is evaluated in R once, in batches
of \code{chunk_size} rows, and written with the columns of the remote data to
a temporary table (one pass over the data, and a copy of it in the
database), whose columns the design then reads like any others; a
message names the first such term. The expression is evaluated on the
rows where its input columns are present, and a row where it gives \code{NA}
is a row with a missing value. With \code{engine = "sql"}, such a formula is
refused instead. \code{summary(D)} shows, for each term, whether SQL computes
it, and \code{summary(D, sql = TRUE)} the generated SQL.

A term whose factors have more than about eight million combined
level-column cells is refused; for a factor with many levels, use fixed
effects (\code{y ~ x | f} in \code{\link[=design_lm]{design_lm()}} and \code{\link[=design_glm]{design_glm()}}).
}

\section{Operations}{

The operations of a design in memory (see \link{design_algebra} and
\link{design_arithmetic}), computed in the database:
\itemize{
\item \code{crossprod(D)}: \verb{X'X}; \code{crossprod(D, "y")}: \verb{X'y} for one or more
columns of the remote data (or a \code{DBI::SQL()} expression).
\item Scaled rows: \code{"w" * D} (a column name), \code{DBI::SQL("1 / v") * D} (an
expression over the columns), \code{2 * D}, \code{D / "w"} and \code{-D} scale each
row, as \code{w * D} with a numeric vector in memory. So \code{crossprod(D, "w" * D)} is \verb{X'WX}, \code{crossprod("w" * D, "y")} is \verb{X'Wy} and \code{colSums("w" * D, group = "g")} the weighted sums within groups. Only one design is
involved: \code{crossprod(x, y)} needs \code{y} to be \code{x} with other scales.
\item \code{colSums(D)}, \code{colMeans(D)}, and with \code{group = "g"} (a column, or a
\code{DBI::SQL()} expression), the sums or means within groups (sorted as
\code{\link[base:rowsum]{base::rowsum()}} sorts them).
\item \code{D \%*\% beta}, \code{rowSums(D)}, \code{rowMeans(D)} and
\code{quadratic_diagonal(D, V)}: a remote column (\link{design_column}), a
lazy SQL expression over the rows of the design, where a design in
memory returns a vector. Arithmetic on it builds another column;
\code{as.vector(p)} reads its values into R, \code{as.data.frame(p, keep = "id")}
reads them with columns of the remote data (to join them back), and
\code{\link[=compute]{compute()}} stores it in a temporary table. \code{design_column(D, "y")}
makes a column from a column of the remote data. Columns of one design
combine, separate products included (\code{D \%*\% b1 + D \%*\% b2}): a
product becomes a view only when it is first read, on top of the
products it is combined with (two products each read before they are
combined cannot be).
\item \code{as.matrix(D)} collects the rows and builds the dense matrix, only when
there are at most \code{getOption("design.matrix.remote_max_rows", 1e5)} rows
or with \code{force = TRUE}.
\item \code{design_matrix(D, newdata)} applies the design to a data frame, and
\code{design_matrix(D, con, table = ...)} to other remote data.
\item \code{summary(D)}: the engine and, for each term, whether SQL computes it.
}
}

\section{Compression}{

When the rows have few distinct \emph{design patterns} (the same factor levels
and the same values of the numeric variables of the design, as with
factors and discrete numeric variables), \code{crossprod()}, \code{colSums()},
\code{colMeans()}, \code{\link[=design_lm]{design_lm()}} and \code{\link[=design_glm]{design_glm()}} can compress: the one scan
of the rows also groups by the numeric variables, which returns each
pattern with its number of rows and the sums of the weights and
responses, and the result is computed from the design of the patterns,
in memory. The option \code{design.matrix.remote_compress} controls it.
\code{TRUE} always compresses (unless every row is distinct); \code{FALSE} never
does. With \code{"auto"} (default), the choice depends on the operation:
\itemize{
\item \code{colSums()} and \code{colMeans()} are never compressed (grouping by the
numeric variables costs more than it saves);
\item \code{crossprod()} is compressed only for designs with many numeric columns
(currently at least 8 distinct products of numeric variables; the
threshold is provisional), where compression saves many per-row
products, and when the patterns are at most 1\% of the rows. Other
designs skip the decision entirely;
\item \code{\link[=design_glm]{design_glm()}} is compressed when the patterns are at most 20\% of the
rows (each IRLS iteration then reads the patterns instead of the rows);
\item \code{\link[=design_lm]{design_lm()}} without weights is compressed when the patterns are at
most 0.1\% of the rows;
\item weighted \code{\link[=design_lm]{design_lm()}} is compressed only for designs with many numeric
columns (as \code{crossprod()}), when the patterns are at most 0.1\% of the
rows (the sum of squares within the patterns takes a second pass over
the rows).
}

These thresholds are provisional. The option
\code{design.matrix.remote_compress_ratio} (unset by default), when set, caps
every one of these ratios: under \code{"auto"}, the patterns must then also be
at most that fraction of the rows.

In every case the number of patterns must also be at most
\code{getOption("design.matrix.remote_compress_max", 1e6)}. To decide, a design
with a continuous numeric column (a \code{DOUBLE} column with many distinct
values in a sample of the rows) is not compressed, at almost no cost;
otherwise the number of patterns is estimated over all the rows
(\code{approx_count_distinct()}). Designs that keep rows with missing values
(\code{na.action = na.pass}) are not compressed.

Remote designs keep only the \emph{Algebra} promise of the package: results
agree with the in-memory computation up to floating point rounding. With
infinite values in the data, the placement of \code{NaN} and \code{Inf} in sums can
differ from the dense computation, and \code{NaN} may appear where the dense
result has \code{NA}.
}

\section{Arguments of remote data}{

With remote data, \code{\link[=design_matrix]{design_matrix()}}, \code{\link[=design_lm]{design_lm()}}, \code{\link[=design_glm]{design_glm()}} and
\code{predict()} take, through \code{...}: \code{table} or \code{sql} (the remote rows: a
table name, or a \code{SELECT} query; not with Parquet paths), \code{engine}
(\code{"auto"}, or \code{"sql"} to refuse variables that must be evaluated in R)
and \code{chunk_size} (rows per batch when variables are evaluated in R,
default \code{getOption("design.matrix.chunk_size", 250000)}).
}

\section{Linear models}{

\code{\link[=design_lm]{design_lm()}} works on remote data as on a data frame:
\code{design_lm(y ~ x + f, con, table = "flights", weights = w, subset = year > 2000)},
or on a remote design: \code{design_lm(D, y = "col", weights = "w")}.
\code{weights}, \code{offset}, \code{subset} and \code{y} are column names (strings or bare
names), \code{DBI::SQL()} expressions, or R expressions over the columns, as
are the response and \code{offset()} terms of the formula. R expressions with
a SQL translation (arithmetic as for the SQL engine; for \code{subset}, also
comparisons, \code{&}, \code{|}, \code{!}, \code{\%in\%} with constants and \code{is.na()}) run in
the database. Any other R expression, such as \code{weights = myfun(x)}, is
evaluated in R, batch by batch, and its values are written with the
columns of the remote data to a temporary table, on which the fit runs
(one more pass over the data, with a message naming the expression;
\code{engine = "sql"} refuses). Compression stays exact, since the computed values are columns
of that table. \code{na.action} can be \code{\link[=na.omit]{na.omit()}} (default), \code{\link[=na.exclude]{na.exclude()}},
\code{\link[=na.fail]{na.fail()}} or \code{\link[=na.pass]{na.pass()}}, with the results or errors of \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}} (with
fixed effects, \code{na.omit} or \code{na.exclude}; other na.action functions are
refused, in memory too). The
rows used are those with no missing model variable, response, weight or
offset, and factor levels are those of these rows, as in memory.

The fit is the same algorithm as in memory, on remote primitives: with
few design patterns it is compressed (see \emph{Compression}); otherwise it
runs \link[=design_qr]{qr()} of the remote design (\verb{X'WX} in the database,
columns judged with \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}}'s rule, suspect columns from products with
the data), with the factor's values computed with numeric variables
shifted when that is an exact transform of the design (so that \code{x + 1e7}
keeps its accuracy). Coefficients, aliased columns, covariance, residual
standard error, degrees of freedom, \code{logLik()}, \code{AIC()}, \code{BIC()} and
\code{summary()} are those of \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}} on the same rows, up to rounding.

Results that have one value per row are tables in the database (names of
temporary views, of class \code{dm_remote_table}, with all the columns of the
remote data): \code{fitted(fit)} (columns \code{.fitted} and \code{.resid}; with
\code{na.exclude}, the rows with missing values too, with \code{NULL}), and
\code{predict(fit)} or \code{predict(fit, newdata = con, table = ...)} (column
\code{.fitted}; with \code{se.fit = TRUE}, \code{.se.fit}, and the attributes \code{df} and
\code{residual.scale}; with intervals, \code{.lwr} and \code{.upr}; for a rank-deficient
fit, \code{.nonestim}, the non-estimable cases flagged by \code{rankdeficient} as in
\code{\link[stats:predict.lm]{stats::predict.lm()}}, with its warnings), computed by the same code as
in memory, in SQL.
\code{residuals(fit)} returns the remote design of the fit with two more
columns, \code{.fitted} and \code{.resid}, which can scale its rows: for
heteroskedasticity-consistent and clustered standard errors,

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{R <- residuals(fit)
meat_hc1 <- crossprod(R, DBI::SQL('".resid" * ".resid"') * R)
scores <- colSums(".resid" * R, group = "cluster")
meat_cl <- crossprod(scores)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

(with \code{bread <- vcov(fit) / sigma(fit)^2}, the HC1 covariance is
\code{n / (n - k) * bread \%*\% meat_hc1 \%*\% bread}).

\strong{Differences from in-memory fits.}
\itemize{
\item \code{model.frame()} is not available for a remote fit, and \code{model} is
ignored: the data stay in the database (\code{fit$data$design} is the
remote design of the fit).
\item Results with one value per row are tables in the database, not
vectors: \code{fitted()} (\code{.fitted}, \code{.resid}), \code{predict()} (\code{.fitted};
\code{se.fit} as a column \code{.se.fit} with attributes \code{df} and
\code{residual.scale}, instead of a list; \code{.nonestim} instead of the
attribute \code{"non-estim"}), and \code{residuals()}, a remote design.
\item These tables have no row order: rows are matched to the data through
its columns, which every table keeps.
\item \code{compress = "auto"} decides from other evidence than in memory (see
\emph{Compression}); the results are the same either way.
}
}

\section{GLMs}{

\code{\link[=design_glm]{design_glm()}} accepts remote data like \code{\link[=design_lm]{design_lm()}}:
\code{design_glm(y ~ x + f, binomial, con, table = "d", weights = w, offset = log(e))},
with \code{weights} and \code{offset} column names, \code{DBI::SQL()} or R expressions
over the columns, \code{offset()} terms in the formula, and a remote design
with \code{y} naming the response column (two columns for successes and
failures). The fit is the same code as in memory, on the remote methods
of the products: with few design patterns (see \emph{Compression}), the
iterations run on the patterns; otherwise each iteration computes its
products in the database. \code{predict(fit, newdata = con, table = ..., type = "response")}, \code{fitted()} and \code{residuals(fit, type = )} return a temporary view with a \code{.fitted} (and \code{.resid}) column next to
the columns of the remote data. Fixed effects (\code{y ~ x | fe}, see
\code{\link[=design_glm]{design_glm()}}) work remotely as in memory: each iteration's sums over
rows are queries, the working weights and responses a temporary table.
}

\examples{
if (requireNamespace("duckdb", quietly = TRUE)) {
    con <- DBI::dbConnect(duckdb::duckdb())
    d <- data.frame(y = rnorm(100), x = rnorm(100), f = gl(4, 25))
    DBI::dbWriteTable(con, "d", d)
    D <- design_matrix(y ~ x * f, con, table = "d")
    D
    beta <- solve(crossprod(D), crossprod(D, "y"))
    p <- D \%*\% beta
    p
    head(as.vector(p))
    r <- design_column(D, "y") - p
    head(as.data.frame(r, nm = "resid"))
    summary(D)
    DBI::dbDisconnect(con)
}
}
